WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:03.020
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.660
The fourteenth film in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, Doctor Strange brings the eponymous

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.880
superhero's origin tale to the big screen
and, at the same time, sneaks in a good number

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.280
of easter eggs to the comic books and the
wider MCU.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.520
In this video, I've put together all the easter
eggs and references I could spot in the movie.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.490
As always, do let me know in the comments
about any other cool easter eggs you spotted.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:27.140
Just before I get stuck in, I'm running a
giveaway for this awesome hardback book from

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.029
DK called "The Mysterious World of Doctor
Strange".

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:35.580
It's packed with over 170 pages of illustrations
and showcases many of the weird and wonderful

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.080
characters, monsters, demons and demi-gods
that have appeared in Marvel's Doctor Strange

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.080
comics over the years.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.860
For a chance to win a copy, all you have to
do is subscribe and leave a comment or reply

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.040
to a comment on this video.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.660
And just a quick warning that there will be
spoilers in this video, so if you're not ready

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.180
for that, click here to watch my spoiler-free
review of the movie.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.540
The art of Steve Ditko's Doctor Strange comics
was a major influence and reference point

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.440
for the look of the Dark Dimension and the
Multiverse that we see during Stephen Strange's

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.290
Magical Mystery Tour and during his confrontation
with Dormammu.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.970
Interestingly over a year was spent on pre-vis
and concept art for the new dimensions that

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.140
we see in the film.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.000
And the filmmakers have said they very much
wanted to pay tribute to Ditko's original

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.100
weird and wonderful artwork from the 60s to
make sure their movie was indeed strange.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.990
A more recent comic-book influence was 2007's
The Oath with the movie reflecting the fun

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:25.990
tone of that mini-series.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.240
But there are also some specific easter eggs
to that particular comic-book story arc.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.170
In the movie Christine Palmer has to operate
on a badly injured Strange, just as Night

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.520
Nurse does in the comic.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.220
And in both the movie and the comic there's
a humourous scene where Strange suddenly appears

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.340
as an astral projection of himself during
the procedure.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.590
And the number 280, which appears briefly
on the back of Strange's broken watch, is

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.890
also an easter egg to the comic Doctor Strange
volume 2 issue 80 where Strange materializes

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:55.430
as his astral form to doctors operating on
his injured body.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.400
Unlike the comics, Rachel McAdams's Christine
Palmer doesn't get the moniker of Night Nurse

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.400
in the movie.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.590
In the comics, the name "Night Nurse" is more
of a shared moniker taken on by various characters

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.420
who look after injured superheroes.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.720
In the movie, Christine Palmer is actually
a doctor, and not a nurse, which is perhaps

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.330
a nod to the joke in The Oath comic that another
Night Nurse character makes about why she

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.470
has that alias even though she's actually
a doctor.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:22.260
Still, the film makes a subtle allusion to
the character of Night Nurse as we see Christine

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.970
Palmer become the go-to doctor to treat super-powered
individuals like Doctor Strange and The Ancient

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.010
One when they arrive at the hospital.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.200
And Strange and Palmer's previous romantic
history in the film tips its hat to the romance

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.200
between Doctor Strange and Night Nurse at
the end of The Oath.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.700
By the way, if you enjoy Marvel's Netflix
TV shows, you'll know that there have been

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.090
lots of allusions to Rosario Dawson's character
Claire Temple being Night Nurse.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.500
The character of Nicodemus West is also taken
from The Oath series where, like in the movie,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.310
he's also the doctor who operated on Strange's
hands after his accident.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.210
And Marvel's graphic novel "Into Shamballa"
gets a cheeky easter egg in the form of the

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.190
wifi password at Kamar-Taj!

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.910
Doctor Strange's on-screen origin story borrows
significantly from the comics with his depiction

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:07.300
as an arrogant surgeon who turns down patients,
his car accident and going penniless trying

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.040
to find a cure for his hands until he discovers
Kamar-Taj and The Ancient One.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.880
The address of the New York Sanctum Santorum,
177A Bleecker Street, is a nod to the same

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.750
address in the comics which was actually also
the address of an apartment that Marvel comic

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.480
book writers Roy Thomas and Gary Friedrich
once shared.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.340
Given that by the end of the movie 177A Bleecker
Street becomes the new residence of Doctor

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.520
Strange, it's interesting that it has a similar
ring to Sherlock's 221B Baker Street.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.709
Benedict Cumberbatch, of course plays Sherlock
in the BBC TV series, and I found it intriguing

00:03:36.709 --> 00:03:40.760
that some of the music that plays during the
film and over the credits had a Sherlock theme

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.100
music meets Pink Floyd vibe to it.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.739
And during the scene where Strange is driving
along the winding road, you can clearly hear

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:50.310
Pink Floyd's track Interstellar Overdrive.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.230
It's hardly surprising that their psychedelic
rock features in the movie's soundtrack; after

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:59.370
all back in 1968, the band used artwork from
the comic Strange Tales on the cover of their

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.470
second album, A Saucerful of Secrets!

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:05.910
And, much more recently, Benedict Cumberbatch
actually performed the song Comfortably Numb

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:10.650
on stage in London with former Pink Floyd
guitarist and vocalist David Gilmour.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.520
The Cloak of Levitation from the comics gets
a bit of an update from the iconic comic book

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.639
design though it keeps the main red colour,
losing the gold colours except for the clasps

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:19.819
around the top.

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:24.729
And the cloak's animated character has shades
of the magic carpet from Disney's movie Aladdin

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:25.889
about it.

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:30.440
The Seal of Vishanti appears in the movie
in the skylight for each of the Sanctum Santorums,

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:35.020
on the doors, and on the luminous globe at
Kamar-Taj as well as on the the Eye of Agamotto.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.139
In the comics the Vishanti are a union of
three mystical god-like beings who protect

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:40.139
the Earth.

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:44.610
One of the Vishanti is Agamotto who gets namechecked
in the film as the father of the mystic arts

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.550
and the first Sorcerer Supreme.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:50.770
The amulet, the Eye of Agamotto, is named
after him and in the comics has various powers,

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.789
while in the MCU it's used by Doctor Strange
to manipulate time.

00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:59.099
And at the end of the film Wong reveals that
the Eye is actually one of the six infinity

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:01.360
stones, specifically the Time Stone.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.270
It's curious too that the movie seems to be
setting the MCU up for Thanos to come and

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:10.330
take the infinity stone by just leaving it
in a room that was already busted by Kaecilius

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.289
and his Zealots earlier!

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:16.169
The book from which Kaecilius rips the pages
he needs to summon Dormammu is the Book of

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:20.520
Cagliostro, a nod to the mystical book in
the comics which Mordo used to travel back

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.520
in time.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.819
The Staff of the Living Tribunal, which Mordo
wields while training with Strange, is possibly

00:05:25.819 --> 00:05:30.009
a reference to the Staff of Polar Power from
the comics, which has the ability to absorb

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:31.180
evil magical energy.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.509
But, it's also an easter egg to the Living
Tribunal, a cosmic being that protects the

00:05:35.509 --> 00:05:36.789
Multiverse in the comics.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:41.550
This is the first time the name has been mentioned
in the MCU and might have implications for

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.930
Infinity War as in the comics the Living Tribunal
has power that exceeds the Infinity Gauntlet.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:52.039
Mordo's Vaulting Boots of Valtorr are a nod
to the comic book entity Valtorr who grants

00:05:52.039 --> 00:05:54.150
power to anyone who can invoke him.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.520
Thanks to them in the movie, Mordo seems to
be able to leap a great distance.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.669
Wong's weapon that he grabs to defend the
Hong Kong Sanctum is the Wand of Watoomb.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:06.649
We don't really see what it can do in the
movie, but in the comics it can defend against

00:06:06.649 --> 00:06:09.120
mystical attacks as well as open portals to
other dimensions.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.340
The luminous orange-coloured magic that Doctor
Strange uses is derived from an energy force

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.800
called Eldritch light.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.210
It can be harnessed as "slash-and-shield-type
weapons" or as an Eldritch Whip which we often

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:21.580
see him use.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:26.289
And the luminous orange ropes that Kaecilius's
Zealots use to bind the librarian at Kamar-Taj

00:06:26.289 --> 00:06:31.469
in mid-air look like the MCU's version of
the comics' Crimson Bands of Cyttorak, a spell

00:06:31.469 --> 00:06:33.930
that contain a prisoner within its bands.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.589
When Strange tried to run and grab an axe
in the Sanctum during his battle with Kaecilius,

00:06:38.589 --> 00:06:42.860
it's likely a nod to the Axe of Angarruumus,
a weapon that Doctor Strange acquired in the

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.629
2015 relaunch of a new Doctor Strange comic
book series.

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:50.899
And the artefact in the Sanctum which Kaecilius
mocks Strange for not knowing what it does

00:06:50.899 --> 00:06:52.969
is the Brazier of Bom'Galiath.

00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:56.710
In the comics, the brazier allows inter-dimensional
travel between realms.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.449
Master Hamir, who Strange confuses for The
Ancient One upon his arrival at Kamar-Taj,

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:06.279
is an updated version of Hamir from the comics
who was an assistant to the Ancient One.

00:07:06.279 --> 00:07:11.039
The wider MCU also gets a couple of references,
with Wong name-dropping The Avengers as defending

00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:15.539
the world against physical threats, whereas
the Masters safeguard the world against mystical

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:16.539
threats.

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:19.650
We also see the Avengers Tower in some of
the shots of the New York skyline.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:23.729
There's a possible reference to Iron Man 2
during the phone conversation Strange has

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:24.970
while driving.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:30.399
Billy, who is telling Strange about possible
new patients, mentions a "35-year old marine

00:07:30.399 --> 00:07:33.879
colonel, [that] crushed his lower spine in
some kind of experimental armour."

00:07:33.879 --> 00:07:38.099
This could be a reference to the Hammer Tech
test pilot injured when the Iron-Man rip-off

00:07:38.099 --> 00:07:42.750
suit he was wearing twisted 180 degrees on
his upper half while his legs were stuck where

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:43.750
they were.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:47.339
Tony Stark played the video of the injury
during the Senate Armed Forces Committee hearing

00:07:47.339 --> 00:07:48.870
in Iron Man 2.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:52.839
Marvel legend Stan Lee cameos in the scene
where Doctor Strange and Mordo are tipped

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:57.770
sideways onto a bus by Kaecilius as he folds
the street to stop them escaping through a

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:58.770
gateway.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:02.139
Lee is riding on the bus and reading "The
Doors of Perception" by Aldous Huxley, laughing

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:03.629
at the craziness of it all.

00:08:03.629 --> 00:08:07.279
The Doors of Perception is a book about what
Aldous Huxley experienced after taking the

00:08:07.279 --> 00:08:11.699
drug mescaline in 1953, and the reference
is a tongue-in-cheek nod to the psychedelic

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:14.439
aesthetic of the movie and the original Ditko
comics.

00:08:14.439 --> 00:08:18.379
A surprising cameo you might not have noticed
is that Benedict Cumberbatch performed the

00:08:18.379 --> 00:08:20.379
motion capture for Dormammu.

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:23.879
And casting Cumberbatch in the role is kind
of apt when you consider that director Scott

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:29.709
Derrickson said "[Dormammu is] a kind of ultra-inflated
version of Strange […] an ego run amok."

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:33.800
By the way, the design of Dormammu in the
movie with a kind of folded face is a visual

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.150
shoutout to the way that Dormammu was originally
drawn by Ditko with stripes in the character's

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.150
head.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:42.169
There's a small cameo for the character of
Daniel Drumm who is the master of the New

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:45.150
York Sanctum before Kaecilius arrives and
kills him.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:50.240
Daniel Drumm, who is also dead in the comics,
has a brother Jericho, aka Brother Voodoo,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:54.710
who takes on the mantle of Sorcerer Supreme
from Doctor Strange in The New Avengers comics.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:59.160
There's a blink-and-you'll-miss-it cameo for
Tina Minoru, played by Linda Louise Duan.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:03.010
She appears briefly with the Ancient One and
Daniel Drumm, and later momentarily in the

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:04.430
Hong Kong Sanctum.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:08.270
Tina Minoru is the mother of Niko Minoru,
a lead character in Marvel's The Runaways

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:11.130
comics which are being adapted as a Hulu TV
series.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:15.280
Ben Collins, aka "The Stig" from Top Gear,
has a small cameo as the stunt driver for

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.370
Doctor Strange's car crash.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:21.950
The mid-credits scene features a cameo from
Chris Hemsworth as Thor and teases a Doctor

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:24.320
Strange appearance in the upcoming Thor: Ragnarok.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.710
In the scene, Doctor Strange is wearing a
pair of yellow gloves, a hat tip to the golden-coloured

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.860
gloves that Ditko drew for Doctor Strange.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.770
You can also click here for a more detailed
analysis of that scene, or you can check the

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.640
link later in the video description below.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.360
Mordo's turn to the dark side in the post-credits
scene links up the character to his classic

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.750
villain role of Baron Mordo in the comics.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:48.090
And he's dressed in muted green colours, a
hat tip to his comic book counterpart's classic

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:49.370
green costume.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:53.390
There's also more references to Mordo's villainous
background at earlier points in the movie,

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:58.060
for example when Mordo says to the Ancient
One "I wanted the power to defeat my enemies."

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:01.910
And when the Ancient One comments to Strange
that "Mordo's soul was forged by the fires

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:02.910
of his youth".

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:05.971
You can click here for more details on the
post-credits scene and what it means for a

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:09.390
Doctor Strange sequel, and I'll also put a
link in the video description below.

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:13.410
There's a fair bit of product placement in
Doctor Strange, but there's a Marvel-related

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.340
one for Synchrony Bank whose name appears
on the building that gets split and warped

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:20.210
in two during one of the mind-bending action
scenes.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:24.710
Synchrony Bank is Marvel's official partner
and issuer of the Marvel Mastercard!

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:28.630
And finally at over the movie's end credits
we hear the theme called "The Master of the

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:32.430
Mystic End Credits", which is a nod to the
second series of Doctor Strange comics which

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:34.390
were subtitled "Master of the Mystic Arts".

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:38.270
Now, I'd love to hear about any other easter
eggs or references that I missed.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.600
And do let me know what you thought of the
movie.

